WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:05.339
So on this program, I try to strike the right
balance between being complacent and being

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:06.339
alarmist.

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:07.950
I try to avoid being either.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:11.450
I try to just be realistic and kind of give
it to you straight.

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:18.090
Simply telling you that there is concern among
Democrats about Minnesota I think is accurate.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.630
I don't think it's alarmist and I don't think
it's complacent.

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:27.349
Donald Trump, according to some Democratic
strategists, could win the state of Minnesota.

00:00:27.349 --> 00:00:28.749
And you might be worried.

00:00:28.749 --> 00:00:32.500
You might be wondering, Minnesota, are we
should we really be worried about that?

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:33.500
Why?

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:38.400
Well, the truth is that Minnesota hasn't voted
for a Republican presidential candidate since

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:39.820
1972.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.070
That's just shy of 50 years ago.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:45.080
And if you look at the polling, it sort of
looks okay.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:46.080
Right.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.890
I mean, it looks OK for Joe Biden.

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:51.410
Joe Biden is currently leading the polling
in Minnesota by an average of three point

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:52.560
seven.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:58.330
But the problem is that three point seven
really isn't that much to be comfortable with.

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:03.970
Minnesota was actually already almost a problem
for Hillary Clinton in 2016.

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:08.940
Hillary Clinton did win Minnesota in 2016,
but only by about one point five.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:15.240
And if you look further into history, Barack
Obama won Minnesota by eleven.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.520
In 2008, he won it by eight.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:20.520
In 2012, John Kerry won Minnesota by four.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:24.540
In 2004, Al Gore won it only by two in the
year 2000.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:29.831
And so when you see it this close and you
know that there's voter suppression, that's

00:01:29.831 --> 00:01:32.049
not in the polling there, there's meddling.

00:01:32.049 --> 00:01:33.049
That's not in the polling.

00:01:33.049 --> 00:01:36.510
There's all sorts of things that are not reflected
by the polling.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:41.460
I am not particularly comfortable with Joe
Biden being up by only three point seven in

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:42.740
the state of Minnesota.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:46.510
Now, there's another reason that Minnesota
could be a problem.

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:50.260
And Minnesota could turn into the Wisconsin
of 2020.

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:56.600
Remember, in 2016, Hillary Clinton was widely
criticized for not spending time in Wisconsin,

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:59.799
a state that she ultimately lost by a very
small margin.

00:01:59.799 --> 00:02:05.150
And it was one of those three states that
flipped the results to Donald Trump electorally.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:08.319
Joe Biden may have a similar situation right
now.

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:13.570
And what I mean by that is in Florida, Joe
Biden is struggling with Latino voters right

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:15.050
now in Florida.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:19.700
And it seems as though Joe Biden is going
to have to devote even more resources to Florida.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:24.030
Florida always a focal point, particularly
for Democrats, because taking Florida away

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:29.570
from the Republican candidate is so powerful
in these electoral races to 270.

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:34.300
But again, if Joe Biden has to put more time
and effort into Florida than he would otherwise

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:38.340
have to ordinarily have to wear it, not for
the Hispanic vote.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:41.940
He may not have the resources or time in Minnesota.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:46.320
And Minnesota is exactly the type of state
that the polling could get wrong.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:52.560
Remember, the 2016 polling was mostly correct
at the national level, at the national level.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:57.390
The polling said Hillary's up three and she
won by more than two in the popular vote.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:01.860
But it is also true that the polling was not
correct in Wisconsin.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:07.370
In Wisconsin, Hillary Clinton in the polling
was winning by six and a half.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:12.010
And you might recall, as I just mentioned
five minutes ago, Donald Trump won Wisconsin

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:13.650
by about three quarters of a point.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.360
The Wisconsin polling was very, very wrong.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:20.379
So what do we do about Minnesota, about all
of these situations?

00:03:20.379 --> 00:03:22.129
Well, we learned from the past.

00:03:22.129 --> 00:03:24.110
We learned from past experiences.

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:29.010
We understand that Democrats are worried about
Minnesota.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:33.780
And so we should be worried about Minnesota
and we should focus on it when Trump advisers

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:38.810
what living backup when Trump publicly says
all our polls look great, we're winning everything.

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:39.810
It's B.S.

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.170
We all know it's B.S.

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:48.110
When privately Trump advisers and Republican
strategists say we're not writing off Michigan.

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:54.510
We understand that Michigan could get complicated
when privately they start looking again at

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:56.209
a state like Minnesota.

00:03:56.209 --> 00:03:57.360
We understand that.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:58.360
We can't ignore it.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:02.190
We never stop the focus on getting out the
vote.

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:07.650
And any one of these states could be the difference
maker now in the coming weeks.

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:12.940
I'm going to show you some of my plausible
electoral maps.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:15.720
Now, why the hell would you listen to me about
that?

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:16.720
You really shouldn't.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:20.130
I got it way wrong in in 2016 and 2016.

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:23.550
I believed the Wisconsin polling and so on
my map.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:26.050
I had Wisconsin definitely for Hillary.

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:27.910
I was wrong.

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:30.600
But I am going to give you some scenarios.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:36.820
And incredibly, there is a scenario for for
this upcoming election that would put the

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:40.770
electoral votes at 269 to 269.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:45.530
It's not the most likely scenario, but it's
also not that unlikely.

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:50.120
And it depends on Maine being split, as they
do in terms of their electoral votes.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:51.940
So we will get to that.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:52.850
We have plenty of time for that.

